Translation patch: keystore2::utils::get_current_time_in_milliseconds (macOS host only)

Change: keystore2/src/utils.rs: on Apple hosts the call reads `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`; on every other host it still reads `CLOCK_BOOTTIME`.
Why: the `libc` crate defines `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` for Linux and Android, not for macOS, so `keystore2` does not compile on a macOS host.
Behaviour: on macOS the clock does not count time the machine spends asleep, which `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` does. A Linux host compiles the original line.
Classification: B
Assumptions: the clock is an oracle on the host; a result obtained on macOS says nothing about the Android clock.
Obligations: an equivalence-gate packet before this patch is listed in any series that is extracted.
Evidence: none yet; this patch is not in translation-patches/series.
Independent: yes

diff --git a/keystore2/src/utils.rs b/keystore2/src/utils.rs
index b27a3c0..f06583b 100644
--- a/keystore2/src/utils.rs
+++ b/keystore2/src/utils.rs
@@ -516,7 +516,7 @@ pub fn get_current_time_in_milliseconds() -> i64 {
     let mut current_time = libc::timespec { tv_sec: 0, tv_nsec: 0 };
     // SAFETY: The pointer is valid because it comes from a reference, and clock_gettime doesn't
     // retain it beyond the call.
-    unsafe { libc::clock_gettime(libc::CLOCK_BOOTTIME, &mut current_time) };
+    unsafe { #[cfg(target_vendor = "apple")] use libc::CLOCK_MONOTONIC as CLOCK_BOOTTIME; #[cfg(not(target_vendor = "apple"))] use libc::CLOCK_BOOTTIME; libc::clock_gettime(CLOCK_BOOTTIME, &mut current_time) };
     current_time.tv_sec as i64 * 1000 + (current_time.tv_nsec as i64 / 1_000_000)
 }
 
